<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Section 1: Review of Lessons 5–6</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 1: Review of Lessons 5–6</h3>
<p><strong>Quick Summary</strong></p>
<p>In Lessons 5 and 6, we expanded our vocabulary exponentially by
learning how Semitic roots generate new meanings through derived stems.
The <strong>Aphel</strong> stem (prefix <em>ʾa-</em>) generates
causative verbs (e.g., God <em>ʾaḥyī</em> “gave life”). The
<strong>Paael</strong> stem (doubled middle radical) generates intensive
or factitive verbs (e.g., <em>qaddaš</em> “he made holy”).</p>
<p><strong>5-Question Warm-Up Drill</strong></p>
<p><em>(Answers in Section 9)</em></p>
<ol type="1">
<li>What stem does a verb belonging to the pattern <em>ʾaCCeC</em>
belong to (e.g., <em>ʾaqreb</em>)?</li>
<li>What consonant prefix marks the active participle of <em>all</em>
derived stems (Aphel, Paael, etc.)?</li>
<li>Translate: <em>Alāhā ʾaglī šrārā</em>.</li>
<li>If <em>q-d-š</em> in Peal means “to be holy”, what does it mean in
Paael (<em>qaddaš</em>)?</li>
<li>How would you recognize a Paael participle versus a Peal
participle?</li>
</ol>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 1</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Warm-up 1. A verb of the pattern <i>ʾaCCeC</i> (e.g. <i>ʾaqreb</i>) belongs to which stem?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Aphel (causative)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Paael (intensive)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Paael doubles the middle letter and has no ʾa-.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Peal (basic)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Peal has no prefix.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Ethpeel (passive)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The passive stems use ʾet-.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 2</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Warm-up 2. Which consonant prefix marks the active participle of every derived stem?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Mīm (m-), e.g. <i>maʾlep</i>, <i>mqaddeš</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Alaph (ʾa-)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[ʾa- marks the Aphel perfect.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Nun (n-)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[n- is an imperfect prefix.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[Taw (t-)]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[t- is an imperfect prefix.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 3</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Warm-up 3. Translate: <i>Alāhā ʾaglī šrārā</i>.]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[God revealed the truth.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[God is the truth.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>ʾaglī</i> is the verb 'revealed'.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The truth revealed God.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>Alāhā</i> is the subject.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[God sent the truth.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA['Sent' is <i>šadar</i>.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 4</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Warm-up 4. If <i>q-d-š</i> in the Peal means 'to be holy', what does the Paael <i>qaddaš</i> mean?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[to make holy, sanctify]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct: the factitive Paael.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[to be very holy]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Paael here is factitive, not just intensive.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[to be made holy]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That is the passive Ethpaael.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[to cause to be holy through another]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[That would be the causative Aphel.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 5</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Warm-up 5. How do you tell a Paael participle from a Peal participle?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Paael has the m- prefix (<i>mqaddeš</i>); the Peal has none (<i>kāteb</i>).]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Peal has the m- prefix; the Paael has none.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Reversed.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[The Paael ends in -ā; the Peal does not.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[-ā marks the feminine in both.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[They look the same; only context tells.]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The m- prefix distinguishes them.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Section 2: The Weak Verb Challenge — Overview</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 2: The Weak Verb Challenge — Overview</h3>
<p>Until now, we have mostly dealt with “strong” roots (like
<em>k-t-b</em> or <em>š-b-q</em>), where all three consonants boldly
assert themselves in every conjugation. However, some of the most common
verbs in human language—to say, to be, to know, to give, to come—are
“weak”. This means one of their three root consonants is a “weak letter”
(Alaph, Waw, or Yodh), which tends to assimilate, drop out entirely, or
turn into a vowel depending on the surrounding sounds.</p>
<p><strong>Cross-Linguistic Comparison: Weak Consonants</strong></p>
<p>Weak verbs are the bane of every Semitic language student, but they
follow highly predictable phonetic rules.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Hebrew</strong>: I-Guttural (אָמַר <em>ʾāmar</em> = he said);
I-Yod (יָדַע <em>yādaʿ</em> = he knew); Lamed-He/Yod (בָּנָה <em>bānāh</em> =
he built).</li>
<li><strong>Arabic</strong>: Hollow verbs (قَالَ <em>qāla</em> = he said);
Defective/Final Weak (رَمَى <em>ramā</em> = he threw).</li>
<li><strong>Syriac</strong>:
<ul>
<li><strong>I-Alaph</strong>: Root <em>ʾ-m-r</em> (to say) <span
class="math inline">→</span> <em>ʾemar</em> (not <em>ʾamar</em>).</li>
<li><strong>I-Yodh</strong>: Root <em>y-d-ʿ</em> (to know) <span
class="math inline">→</span> <em>īdaʿ</em> (Yodh acts like an <em>i</em>
vowel).</li>
<li><strong>III-Yodh</strong>: Root <em>ḥ-z-y</em> (to see) <span
class="math inline">→</span> <em>ḥzā</em> (the Yodh drops out, leaving
an <em>ā</em> vowel).</li>
</ul></li>
</ul>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Section 3: Morphology of Weak Roots (Peal Perfect)</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 3: Morphology of Weak Roots (Peal Perfect)</h3>
<p>Despite the spelling changes, weak verbs function syntactically
exactly like strong verbs: VSO word order, taking suffixes, and forming
the spine of clauses.</p>
<p><strong>1. I-Alaph Verbs (First consonant is Alaph)</strong></p>
<p>The most famous is <em>ʾ-m-r</em> (“to say”). In the Peal perfect 3rd
masc singular, the vowel shifts to <em>e</em>:</p>
<ul>
<li><em>ʾemar</em> (he said), rather than <em>ʾamar</em>.</li>
<li><em>ʾemret</em> (I said), rather than <em>ʾamret</em>.</li>
</ul>
<p><strong>2. I-Yodh Verbs (First consonant is Yodh)</strong></p>
<p>Very common verbs like <em>y-d-ʿ</em> (to know) and <em>y-t-b</em>
(to sit). The initial <code>y</code> fuses with the preceding invisible
glottal stop to form an <em>ī</em> vowel.</p>
<ul>
<li><em>y-d-ʿ</em> <span class="math inline">→</span> <em>īdaʿ</em> (he
knew).</li>
<li><em>y-t-b</em> <span class="math inline">→</span> <em>īteb</em> (he
sat). <em>Note: The verb y-h-b (to give) is highly irregular and behaves
like a strong verb without an internal vowel: yahb (he gave).</em></li>
</ul>
<p><strong>3. III-Yodh Verbs (Third consonant is Yodh)</strong></p>
<p>Verbs ending in Yodh (like <em>ḥ-z-y</em>, <em>b-n-y</em>,
<em>ḥ-y-y</em>). The Yodh falls away in the 3rd masc singular, leaving a
final <em>ā</em> vowel. In other persons, the Yodh can reappear as
heavily vocalized endings.</p>
<ul>
<li><em>ḥ-z-y</em> (to see) <span class="math inline">→</span>
<em>ḥzā</em> (he saw) [looks like a 2-consonant word].</li>
<li><em>ḥ-z-y</em> + <em>-et</em> (I saw) <span
class="math inline">→</span> <em>ḥzēt</em> (the <em>ay</em> contracts to
<em>ē</em>).</li>
<li><em>ḥ-z-y</em> + <em>-ū</em> (They saw) <span
class="math inline">→</span> <em>ḥzaw</em> (the <em>ay-ū</em> contracts
to <em>aw</em>).</li>
</ul>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
