<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 6</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[What does the final Yodh of a III-Yodh root become in the 3 m.sg. perfect?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[A long ā: <i>ḥ-z-y</i> → <i>ḥzā</i> 'he saw']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[It stays as ī: <i>ḥzī</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Not in the 3 m.sg.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[It doubles: <i>ḥazzī</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Doubling marks the Paael.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[It disappears with no vowel: <i>ḥz</i>]]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[The ā remains.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
<question type="description"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Section 5: The highly irregular verbs ʾ-t-y and h-w-y</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[<h3>Section 5: The highly irregular verbs ʾ-t-y and h-w-y</h3>
<p>Two of the most common verbs in Syriac are doubly weak and highly
irregular.</p>
<ol type="1">
<li><strong>ʾ-t-y</strong> (to come): I-Alaph AND III-Yodh.
<ul>
<li>3 m.s: <em>ʾetā</em> (he came).</li>
<li>1 c.s: <em>ʾetēt</em> (I came).</li>
<li>3 m.pl: <em>ʾetaw</em> (they came).</li>
</ul></li>
<li><strong>h-w-y</strong> (to be/become): Used as an auxiliary verb, or
to mean “it happened”. III-Yodh.
<ul>
<li>3 m.s: <em>hwā</em> (he was / it happened).</li>
<li>1 c.s: <em>hwēt</em> (I was).</li>
<li>3 m.pl: <em>hwaw</em> (they were).</li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<hr />

]]></text></questiontext></question>
<question type="multichoice"><name><text>Module 2 · Lesson 7 · Check 7</text></name><questiontext format="html"><text><![CDATA[Which two verbs does the lesson single out as highly irregular?]]></text></questiontext><generalfeedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[]]></text></generalfeedback><single>true</single><shuffleanswers>true</shuffleanswers><answer fraction="100" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>ʾ-t-y</i> 'come' and <i>h-w-y</i> 'be']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Correct.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>k-t-b</i> 'write' and <i>q-ṭ-l</i> 'kill']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[Those are strong roots.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>ʾ-m-r</i> 'say' and <i>ʾ-z-l</i> 'go']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[These are I-Alaph roots, but regular in pattern.]]></text></feedback></answer><answer fraction="0" format="html"><text><![CDATA[<i>y-d-ʿ</i> 'know' and <i>y-h-b</i> 'give']]></text><feedback format="html"><text><![CDATA[These are I-Yodh roots, not the singled-out pair.]]></text></feedback></answer></question>
